kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":26,"beg":{"line":936,"col":4},"end":{"line":936,"col":43}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"array_mut","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[31166,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Free":0}},{"HashConsedValue":[31165,{"Slice":{"Deduplicated":31038}}]},"Mut"]}]}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":26,"beg":{"line":938,"col":4},"end":{"line":938,"col":26}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"initialized","ty":{"Deduplicated":1677}}]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":24,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[0,16],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,null,{"def_id":107,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["pin",0]},{"Ident":["Pin",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":88,"beg":{"line":1092,"col":0},"end":{"line":1092,"col":19}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A pointer which pins its pointee in place."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Pin`] is a wrapper around some kind of pointer `Ptr` which makes that pointer \"pin\" its"},{"DocComment":" pointee value in place, thus preventing the value referenced by that pointer from being moved"},{"DocComment":" or otherwise invalidated at that place in memory unless it implements [`Unpin`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" *See the [`pin` module] documentation for a more thorough exploration of pinning.*"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Pinning values with [`Pin<Ptr>`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In order to pin a value, we wrap a *pointer to that value* (of some type `Ptr`) in a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`]. [`Pin<Ptr>`] can wrap any pointer type, forming a promise that the **pointee**"},{"DocComment":" will not be *moved* or [otherwise invalidated][subtle-details]. If the pointee value's type"},{"DocComment":" implements [`Unpin`], we are free to disregard these requirements entirely and can wrap any"},{"DocComment":" pointer to that value in [`Pin`] directly via [`Pin::new`]. If the pointee value's type does"},{"DocComment":" not implement [`Unpin`], then Rust will not let us use the [`Pin::new`] function directly and"},{"DocComment":" we'll need to construct a [`Pin`]-wrapped pointer in one of the more specialized manners"},{"DocComment":" discussed below."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" We call such a [`Pin`]-wrapped pointer a **pinning pointer** (or pinning ref, or pinning"},{"DocComment":" [`Box`], etc.) because its existence is the thing that is pinning the underlying pointee in"},{"DocComment":" place: it is the metaphorical \"pin\" securing the data in place on the pinboard (in memory)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It is important to stress that the thing in the [`Pin`] is not the value which we want to pin"},{"DocComment":" itself, but rather a pointer to that value! A [`Pin<Ptr>`] does not pin the `Ptr` but rather"},{"DocComment":" the pointer's ***pointee** value*."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The most common set of types which require pinning related guarantees for soundness are the"},{"DocComment":" compiler-generated state machines that implement [`Future`] for the return value of"},{"DocComment":" `async fn`s. These compiler-generated [`Future`]s may contain self-referential pointers, one"},{"DocComment":" of the most common use cases for [`Pin`]. More details on this point are provided in the"},{"DocComment":" [`pin` module] docs, but suffice it to say they require the guarantees provided by pinning to"},{"DocComment":" be implemented soundly."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This requirement for the implementation of `async fn`s means that the [`Future`] trait"},{"DocComment":" requires all calls to [`poll`] to use a <code>self: [Pin]\\<&mut Self></code> parameter instead"},{"DocComment":" of the usual `&mut self`. Therefore, when manually polling a future, you will need to pin it"},{"DocComment":" first."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You may notice that `async fn`-sourced [`Future`]s are only a small percentage of all"},{"DocComment":" [`Future`]s that exist, yet we had to modify the signature of [`poll`] for all [`Future`]s"},{"DocComment":" to accommodate them. This is unfortunate, but there is a way that the language attempts to"},{"DocComment":" alleviate the extra friction that this API choice incurs: the [`Unpin`] trait."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The vast majority of Rust types have no reason to ever care about being pinned. These"},{"DocComment":" types implement the [`Unpin`] trait, which entirely opts all values of that type out of"},{"DocComment":" pinning-related guarantees. For values of these types, pinning a value by pointing to it with a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] will have no actual effect."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The reason this distinction exists is exactly to allow APIs like [`Future::poll`] to take a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] as an argument for all types while only forcing [`Future`] types that actually"},{"DocComment":" care about pinning guarantees pay the ergonomics cost. For the majority of [`Future`] types"},{"DocComment":" that don't have a reason to care about being pinned and therefore implement [`Unpin`], the"},{"DocComment":" <code>[Pin]\\<&mut Self></code> will act exactly like a regular `&mut Self`, allowing direct"},{"DocComment":" access to the underlying value. Only types that *don't* implement [`Unpin`] will be restricted."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value of a type that implements [`Unpin`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If the type of the value you need to \"pin\" implements [`Unpin`], you can trivially wrap any"},{"DocComment":" pointer to that value in a [`Pin`] by calling [`Pin::new`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Create a value of a type that implements `Unpin`"},{"DocComment":" let mut unpin_future = std::future::ready(5);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin it by creating a pinning mutable reference to it (ready to be `poll`ed!)"},{"DocComment":" let my_pinned_unpin_future: Pin<&mut _> = Pin::new(&mut unpin_future);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value inside a [`Box`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The simplest and most flexible way to pin a value that does not implement [`Unpin`] is to put"},{"DocComment":" that value inside a [`Box`] and then turn that [`Box`] into a \"pinning [`Box`]\" by wrapping it"},{"DocComment":" in a [`Pin`]. You can do both of these in a single step using [`Box::pin`]. Let's see an"},{"DocComment":" example of using this flow to pin a [`Future`] returned from calling an `async fn`, a common"},{"DocComment":" use case as described above."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" async fn add_one(x: u32) -> u32 {"},{"DocComment":"     x + 1"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Call the async function to get a future back"},{"DocComment":" let fut = add_one(42);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin the future inside a pinning box"},{"DocComment":" let pinned_fut: Pin<Box<_>> = Box::pin(fut);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you have a value which is already boxed, for example a [`Box<dyn Future>`][Box], you can pin"},{"DocComment":" that value in-place at its current memory address using [`Box::into_pin`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":" use std::future::Future;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" async fn add_one(x: u32) -> u32 {"},{"DocComment":"     x + 1"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" fn boxed_add_one(x: u32) -> Box<dyn Future<Output = u32>> {"},{"DocComment":"     Box::new(add_one(x))"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let boxed_fut = boxed_add_one(42);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin the future inside the existing box"},{"DocComment":" let pinned_fut: Pin<Box<_>> = Box::into_pin(boxed_fut);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are similar pinning methods offered on the other standard library smart pointer types"},{"DocComment":" as well, like [`Rc`] and [`Arc`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value on the stack using [`pin!`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are some situations where it is desirable or even required (for example, in a `#[no_std]`"},{"DocComment":" context where you don't have access to the standard library or allocation in general) to"},{"DocComment":" pin a value which does not implement [`Unpin`] to its location on the stack. Doing so is"},{"DocComment":" possible using the [`pin!`] macro. See its documentation for more."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Layout and ABI"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] is guaranteed to have the same memory layout and ABI[^noalias] as `Ptr`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [^noalias]: There is a bit of nuance here that is still being decided about whether the"},{"DocComment":" aliasing semantics of `Pin<&mut T>` should be different than `&mut T`, but this is true as of"},{"DocComment":" today."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`pin!`]: crate::pin::pin \"pin!\""},{"DocComment":" [`Future`]: crate::future::Future \"Future\""},{"DocComment":" [`poll`]: crate::future::Future::poll \"Future::poll\""},{"DocComment":" [`Future::poll`]: crate::future::Future::poll \"Future::poll\""},{"DocComment":" [`pin` module]: self \"pin module\""},{"DocComment":" [`Rc`]: ../../std/rc/struct.Rc.html \"Rc\""},{"DocComment":" [`Arc`]: ../../std/sync/struct.Arc.html \"A