"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In other words, it links two iterators together, in a chain. 🔗"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`once`] is commonly used to adapt a single value into a chain of"},{"DocComment":" other kinds of iteration."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Basic usage:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let s1 = \"abc\".chars();"},{"DocComment":" let s2 = \"def\".chars();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = s1.chain(s2);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('a'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('b'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('c'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('d'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('e'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some('f'));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Since the argument to `chain()` uses [`IntoIterator`], we can pass"},{"DocComment":" anything that can be converted into an [`Iterator`], not just an"},{"DocComment":" [`Iterator`] itself. For example, arrays (`[T]`) implement"},{"DocComment":" [`IntoIterator`], and so can be passed to `chain()` directly:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a1 = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":" let a2 = [4, 5, 6];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = a1.into_iter().chain(a2);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(1));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(2));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(3));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(4));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(5));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(6));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you work with Windows API, you may wish to convert [`OsStr`] to `Vec<u16>`:"